{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 1, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Tripoli and a Crusader leader. He was the son of Pons of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III succeeded his father in 1152 and ruled until his death in 1187.\n\n* **Early Life and Succession** *\nRaymond III was born around 1130 to Pons of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His mother was a member of the royal family of Jerusalem. He was the heir to the County of Tripoli which was established by his grandfather Raymond IV Count of Toulouse. \n\nIn 1152 Raymond III succeeded his father Pons of Tripoli who died without a male heir. The County of Tripoli was a strategic location for the Crusaders as it controlled the access to the interior of Syria.\n\n* **Crusader and Muslim Relations** *\nRaymond III was a member of the Crusader forces that were based in the County of Tripoli. He was a contemporary of King Amalric I of Jerusalem and King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. Raymond III formed alliances with the Crusaders to counter the Muslim forces in the region. He also married a Muslim woman named Hodierna of Armenia.\n\nIn 1157 Raymond III formed an alliance with King Baldwin III of Jerusalem to attack the Muslim forces in the region. The alliance was successful in capturing the city of Banias from the Muslim forces. \n\nIn 1179 Raymond III signed the Treaty of Turbessel with the Muslim forces in the region. The treaty was a peace agreement between the Crusaders and the Muslim forces in the region.\n\n* **Marriage and Children** *\nRaymond III married Hodierna of Armenia in 1152. Hodierna was a Muslim woman who was a member of the royal family of Armenia. The marriage was a strategic alliance between the Crusaders and the Muslim forces in the region.\n\nRaymond III and Hodierna had two children together. Their son was Bohemond V of Antioch and their daughter was Alice of Tripoli. \n\nRaymond I